// A `for` that goes depth first has one order, an entity and all below it
// before the next: it may read a unique it adds to, and each entity sees
// what those before it added. The same in threads, and along a tree
// whose archetype is sorted by it; and where the `for` reacts, both when
// it goes through everything and when it goes to the few that changed.
// RUN: %ent run %S/Inputs/dfs_count.ent 2>/dev/null > %t.out
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %S/Inputs/dfs_count.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.out
// RUN: sed 's/ tree ordered by / tree sorted ordered by /' \
// RUN:     %S/Inputs/dfs_count.ent > %t.sorted.ent
// RUN: grep -c "tree sorted" %t.sorted.ent | grep -q 1
// RUN: %ent run %t.sorted.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.out
// RUN: %ent run --parallel %t.sorted.ent 2>/dev/null | cmp - %t.out
// RUN: FileCheck %s --input-file=%t.out

// Each box's line is how many came before it; the third has sixty boxes
// in it, which take the lines from 5 on.
// CHECK:      1: line 0: box 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 1: box 2
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 2: box 4
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 3: box 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 4: box 3
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 65: box 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: line 66: box 6
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: 67 lines, sum 294622
// What is below a box before it, counting only some.
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: up 1: box 4
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: up 2: box 2
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: up 3: box 6
// Reacting: all of them the first time, in the same order.
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 1: box 1
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 2: box 2
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 3: box 4
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 4: box 7
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 5: box 3
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 66: box 5
// CHECK-NEXT: 1: changed 67: box 6
// CHECK:      2: 67 lines, sum 294712
// Then the two that changed: the one deeper in the tree first, which
// comes before the other depth first, though after it by depth.
// CHECK:      2: changed 1: box 17
// CHECK-NEXT: 2: changed 2: box 13
